  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "The oracle's temple sat on two faults leaking sweet gas. Delphi, central Greece. Kings and generals came here for over a thousand years.",
        "visual_query": "delphi greece mountain ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "greek ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Pythia sat on a gilded tripod in the inner sanctum, the adyton. She went into a trance. Priests recast what came out as verse.",
        "visual_query": "ancient greek temple columns",
        "visual_fallback": "temple columns"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Plutarch wrote that intoxicating vapours rose from a fissure under the floor. For centuries that was filed away as temple myth. It was not.",
        "visual_query": "ancient greek stone inscription",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In the 1980s a geologist, an archaeologist, a chemist and a toxicologist mapped the site. Two fault lines, Kerna and Delphic, cross directly beneath the sanctum.",
        "visual_query": "rocky limestone mountain fault",
        "visual_fallback": "limestone cliffs"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Below them: bituminous limestone. The gas is ethylene \u2014 sweet-smelling, and in small doses euphoric. Critics still argue the dose. The faults are not in doubt.",
        "visual_query": "greek mountain spring water",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain spring"
      }
    ],
